From 1d00c3edb864a4dceb0a013a215281a2d902cbfc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Currie <rob@example.invalid>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 05:04:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] feat: a clone carries the ProcessID record a TRef in it
 names

A `CloneTree` copies a split collection's rows as the source file wrote them,
so a whole `TRef` member arrives as the file's own description of a record
whose `fPID` is an index among *that* file's `ProcessID` records. The file
the clone went into had none, so a reference read back out of the clone found
neither the object it names nor a process to look it up in and gave nothing:
roottest/root/io/tree/test-rc printed (!NULL!) where ROOT prints N_0_8.

`ClonedSlot` now hands those rows to the writer's own record of a `TRef`
(which streams them, as a filled clone's are streamed) and gives the file
being written a `ProcessID` record for each process they name - the UUID read
from the source file's own record, through `TFile::WriteProcessID` - writing
back the index that file gave it. roottest's create-rc file now holds
`ProcessID0` as ROOT's does, and test-rc's 180 reference lines match the .ref.
The rest of those tests' output is Show's text, which another commit owns.
